From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PrimeWalker <primewalkervn@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 23:41:41 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] Vulkan recorder thread off by default on discrete AMD RDNA 3

On an RX 7800 XT (Navi 32, device 0x747e, driver 26.10.1 LLPC) the device was
lost within seconds of the game window taking focus on every run with the
recorder thread on ("vk: upload submit failed (-4)", breadcrumbs at
submissions 2-36). With TOTK_VK_RECORDER=0 the same build ran without a loss;
TOTK_GPL=0, TOTK_VK_PRESENT=fifo and TOTK_REPLAY_ASYNC=0 each still lost it.

vk_caps.amd_rdna3 marks discrete Navi 31/32/33 (0x7440-0x749f) on AMD's own
driver; the recorder defaults off there only. RDNA 4 (RX 9070), which gains
from the recorder, and every other GPU keep it on. TOTK_VK_RECORDER=1 still
forces it on.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 docs/architecture.md | 2 +-
 runtime/vk.h         | 1 +
 runtime/vk_caps.c    | 4 ++++
 runtime/vk_rec.c     | 8 +++++++-
 4 files changed, 13 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/docs/architecture.md b/docs/architecture.md
index 12f75987adabc962a6151fd417e1f9c89e055a87..6976e7160b4ca3024b30fc8fb6519190e3e09cd9 100644
--- a/docs/architecture.md
+++ b/docs/architecture.md
@@ -82,7 +82,7 @@ Every guest thread is a host thread. TotK assumes Horizon's scheduler: three app
 - Vulkan 1.3 back end in place of MacNativeGames' Metal one. At `QueueSubmitCommands` the recorded NVN commands are replayed into Vulkan command buffers (`vk_exec*.c`).
 - Memory pools. Apple's unified memory let MacNativeGames use the game's pools zero-copy. The back end imports the pools as host memory (`VK_EXT_external_memory_host`). On a discrete GPU, pools bound as buffers are mirrored in device-local memory and the pages the CPU wrote (write watch, above) are uploaded before a replay; idle pool regions are made read-only so the write-watch queries skip them (`vk_memory.c`). GPU-rendered textures are written back when the CPU reads them: on a pool invalidate, on a CPU copy, and for small targets once the CPU waits for a fence (`vk_texture.c`).
 - Replay thread. `TOTK_REPLAY_ASYNC=1` moves the replay off the submitting thread onto a queue at most one frame behind the game; reads of GPU results wait for the newest writer instead of draining the queue (`vk_backend.c`). It is off by default because no timing has shown a gain yet.
-- Recorder thread. The Vulkan calls the replay makes into its command buffer are made by a thread of their own (`vk_rec.c`, after Savage Wind Recomp and LAS Recomp): each `vkCmd*` copies its arguments into a packet of a ring and the recorder makes the calls in order; calls into other command buffers are made at once; the recorder also ends the command buffer, and the submit waits until it has. In the town on an RX 9070 the replay takes 17% less time on the submitting thread. `TOTK_VK_RECORDER=0` makes every call on the submitting thread.
+- Recorder thread. The Vulkan calls the replay makes into its command buffer are made by a thread of their own (`vk_rec.c`, after Savage Wind Recomp and LAS Recomp): each `vkCmd*` copies its arguments into a packet of a ring and the recorder makes the calls in order; calls into other command buffers are made at once; the recorder also ends the command buffer, and the submit waits until it has. In the town on an RX 9070 the replay takes 17% less time on the submitting thread. `TOTK_VK_RECORDER=0` makes every call on the submitting thread; it is the default on discrete RDNA 3 (Navi 31/32/33), where an RX 7800 XT lost the device with the recorder on.
 - Uniform snapshot. The game rewrites per-object uniform blocks on other threads soon after it submits the frame
   that reads them, while the replay still records it. At the start of a submission's replay every uniform range it
   binds from CPU-written pools is copied, and the draws bind the copy (`vk_usnap.c`, after MacNativeGames'
diff --git a/runtime/vk.h b/runtime/vk.h
index 80d7bc5b2b37e899ea134fac9aaa0abf06ef0094..0267bf7ed7db35456ef20b6fb53fbab1d379c7c3 100644
--- a/runtime/vk.h
+++ b/runtime/vk.h
@@ -55,6 +55,7 @@ typedef struct {
     int gpl;                           // VK_EXT_graphics_pipeline_library enabled with fast linking (TOTK_GPL=0: off)
     int amd;                           // an AMD GPU (vendor 0x1002), any driver
     int amd_sc;                        // AMD's Windows driver branch for RDNA 1 and 2 ("AMD proprietary shader compiler")
+    int amd_rdna3;                     // a discrete RDNA 3 GPU (Navi 31/32/33, device 0x7440-0x749f) on AMD's own driver
 } VkCaps;
 extern VkCaps vk_caps;
 void vk_caps_driver(VkPhysicalDevice p);             // first, before vkCreateDevice: vk_caps.amd, amd_sc
diff --git a/runtime/vk_caps.c b/runtime/vk_caps.c
index 97f91e15a892cdc786087df604434d0a2bf1ed11..920f777c298b93ba2a8a57ba6867867a4ffa592d 100644
--- a/runtime/vk_caps.c
+++ b/runtime/vk_caps.c
@@ -44,6 +44,10 @@ void vk_caps_driver(VkPhysicalDevice p) {
     // AMD split RDNA 1 and 2 off into a driver branch of their own; its driverInfo reads
     // "26.10.1 (AMD proprietary shader compiler)" where the RDNA 3 and 4 driver names LLPC.
     vk_caps.amd_sc = drv.driverID == VK_DRIVER_ID_AMD_PROPRIETARY && strstr(drv.driverInfo, "shader compiler");
+    // Discrete RDNA 3 (Navi 31 0x744x, Navi 32 0x746x-0x747x, Navi 33 0x748x-0x749x); RDNA 4 is 0x75xx.
+    uint32_t id = p2.properties.deviceID;
+    vk_caps.amd_rdna3 = vk_caps.amd && !vk_caps.amd_sc && drv.driverID == VK_DRIVER_ID_AMD_PROPRIETARY &&
+                        id >= 0x7440 && id <= 0x749f;
 }
 
 // TOTK_GPL=0/1 forces graphics pipeline libraries off or on. Default off on AMD's RDNA 1/2 driver branch: every boot
diff --git a/runtime/vk_rec.c b/runtime/vk_rec.c
index 811ca1a7f88bcd6720acf5a63f58f92b498826a6..568054bfe18494477ea48cf4c0c260501ee65fdf 100644
--- a/runtime/vk_rec.c
+++ b/runtime/vk_rec.c
@@ -130,7 +130,13 @@ void rec_begin(VkCommandBuffer cb) {
         // Default on for every GPU. AMD drivers once lost the device with the recorder (issues #5 and #40); after
         // vk_mem_note_recording kept objects alive until their submission completed and the recorder began ending
         // its own parts (rec_end_cb), RDNA 1 and 2 cards ran with it on and off without a loss or a white scene.
-        mode = rt_knob("VK_RECORDER", "1");
+        // Discrete RDNA 3 still loses the device with it: an RX 7800 XT (driver 26.10.1, LLPC) lost it within seconds
+        // of the window taking focus on every run with the recorder on, and ran with it off. The default is off
+        // there only; RDNA 4 (RX 9070) runs with it and gains from it. TOTK_VK_RECORDER=1 still forces it on.
+        mode = rt_knob("VK_RECORDER", vk_caps.amd_rdna3 ? "0" : "1");
+        const char *forced = cfg_get("TOTK_VK_RECORDER");
+        if (!mode && vk_caps.amd_rdna3 && !(forced && *forced))
+            rt_log("vk_rec: recorder thread off by default on AMD RDNA 3; TOTK_VK_RECORDER=1 forces it");
         if (mode) {
             plat_event_init(&wake_ev, 0, 0);
             rec_prio = plat_thread_priority_get();
